Great game - best to play Black Mirror II first although I played the third first without any difficulties picking up the previous plot.
Likes:
- complex and dark story line;
- good voice acting;
- puzzles make sense (e.g. the protagonist is a Physics student, and quite a few of his puzzle solving revolve around chemistry and mechanical repair rather than find a special key to fit into a special lock under a special day with full moon etc etc);
- good graphics, considering the game is getting on in age;
- good replay value.
Dislikes:
- sometimes the music get very loud and overwhelming (ok, ok, I get it, something BAD is about to happen!);
- some of the storyline is a tad predictable (clue: evil + Catholic Church = ???)
Otherwise, an excellent game if you are into point and click 3rd person adventure games.

The game type is not only sandbox, but a
sandbox game: indeed it lacks any type (really any type) of goal. The game genre is a strategic medieval city management with stunning graphics and it has (maybe) the better season system I've ever seen.
- 3D graphic and visuals are wonderful;
- music is good also if there are just three or four songs and that's it;
- season system is really realistic and well represented into the game;
- in the first game the morale will be destroyed by death of a family member and the cemetery is used also to replenish it: it's totally realistic;
- trade is another realistic thing: to me is ok but for someone could be too much 'real'.
- total lacking of plot and goals: just survive in a random generated land;
- it gets repetitive: any scenario will force you to find the same configuration of buildings to survive (i.e.: forester+gatherer will be the first of your problems in any scenario also with food buildings). The total absence of research and progression in time will render the game just more repetitive: it's merely the same building after one hour of play and the same after twenty;
- no campaign and no friend or enemies to interact with anyway no interaction at all with anyone;
- after you've decided a scenario is done (because resource is limited or because you're satisfied or because simply you've no more building space) the only strong reason to play another one is a passion for the Steam achievements.
Banished with some more attention and variety could've reached the Olympus of the strategy games. Unfortunately the game is really survive and that's all. Apart from that this game reminds me the first in The Settlers serie: ok in 1993 graphics are not like that :) but the concept is the same. I abandoned The Settlers at the sixth level because I found it repetitive: any new scenario brought more or less the same challenges but at least they are masked with some interaction with enemies and a progression in difficulty. In Banished I felt the same sensation... at the first scenario: for me it's inconceivable to have the same let's say configuration at the first hour and at the tenth. Nothing evolves in Banished.
So to appreciate or not this game depends on the type of player you're: I have limited time to play, I love plot, character/condition evolution, research, development and of course strategy. If you're like me, then you could find this game frustrating and maybe found something better to invest the limited time in.
If you have plenty of time to invest in playing and you cannot sleep until you've obtained every Steam achievement in the universe, then definitely Banished it's for ya ;)

